Tell WTF how many processors this machine has.

numberOfProcessorCores() picks its implementation by operating system, and
the arm that calls sysconf(_SC_NPROCESSORS_ONLN) is spelled as a list of OS
names: LINUX, AIX, OPENBSD, NETBSD, FREEBSD, HAIKU.  OS(LIKEOS) is in none of
them -- and, unlike most of WTF, there is no POSIX arm to fall through to.
The #else is:

    s_numberOfCores = defaultIfUnavailable;   /* 1 */

So every WebKit process on this system believed it was running on a
uniprocessor, whatever the machine actually was, and said so silently.  There
is nothing to detect in the build and no warning at run time; the only visible
trace is a number in webkit://gpu that happens to be smaller than on another
box.  That is what led here: "Threaded rendering: GPU (1 threads)" against
"GPU (2 threads)" on a second virtual machine of the same shape on the same
host.

sysconf(_SC_NPROCESSORS_ONLN) is implemented here (user/lib/libc/src/syscalls/
unistd.c): it counts the bits the kernel puts in the default affinity mask,
which is one per online CPU, and never answers zero.  _SC_NPROCESSORS_CONF
answers the same, CPUs not being taken offline at run time.  So the existing
arm is joined rather than a new one written, exactly as OS(LIKEOS) joins the
LINUX arms in the WTF patches next to this one.

The paint-thread count is the smallest of the things this decides.  With one
core reported:

  - SkiaPaintingEngine::numberOfGPUPaintingThreads() is `cores >= 4 ? 2 : 1',
    so tile painting in the web process ran on a single worker;
  - numberOfCPUPaintingThreads() is max(1, min(8, cores / 2)) -- also 1, which
    is what the software fallback would have got;
  - WorkQueue's concurrent queue is built with `numberOfProcessorCores() - 1'
    workers.  That is ZERO, on a queue whose whole purpose is to run work off
    the calling thread;
  - JavaScriptCore's overrideDefaults() takes its "fewer than four cores"
    garbage-collector tuning, and then the `cores <= 1' branch inside it that
    sets gcIncrementScale to 1 -- the most conservative incremental collection
    it has;
  - WebPageProxy and WebProcessActivityState both gate behaviour on
    `cores > 4', which could not become true.

None of that is a rendering fault and none of it fails visibly.  It is the
engine being told, wrongly, that it has nowhere to put concurrent work.

--- a/Source/WTF/wtf/NumberOfCores.cpp
+++ b/Source/WTF/wtf/NumberOfCores.cpp
@@ -32,7 +32,7 @@
 
 #if OS(DARWIN)
 #include <sys/sysctl.h>
-#elif OS(LINUX) || OS(AIX) || OS(OPENBSD) || OS(NETBSD) || OS(FREEBSD) || OS(HAIKU)
+#elif OS(LINUX) || OS(LIKEOS) || OS(AIX) || OS(OPENBSD) || OS(NETBSD) || OS(FREEBSD) || OS(HAIKU)
 #include <unistd.h>
 #elif OS(QNX)
 #include <sys/syspage.h>
@@ -68,7 +68,7 @@
     int sysctlResult = sysctl(name, sizeof(name) / sizeof(int), &result, &length, 0, 0);
 
     s_numberOfCores = sysctlResult < 0 ? defaultIfUnavailable : result;
-#elif OS(LINUX) || OS(AIX) || OS(OPENBSD) || OS(NETBSD) || OS(FREEBSD) || OS(HAIKU)
+#elif OS(LINUX) || OS(LIKEOS) || OS(AIX) || OS(OPENBSD) || OS(NETBSD) || OS(FREEBSD) || OS(HAIKU)
     long sysconfResult = sysconf(_SC_NPROCESSORS_ONLN);
 
     s_numberOfCores = sysconfResult < 0 ? defaultIfUnavailable : static_cast<int>(sysconfResult);
